Here’s an experiment I particularly like: take the old multiplane camera and make a semantic camera.

Instead of moving through a painted forest, you move through layers representing one event at different timescales: the immediate physical occurrence, an individual’s interpretation, the surrounding culture’s interpretation, and its historical consequences.

You can change perspective without rewriting the underlying event. You can also deliberately change one layer and observe what that changes elsewhere.

Now imagine applying the same operation to sound, memory, interactive fiction, or Pantheon’s History Stereoscope.

And this brings us to something important: historical limitations can become modern creative controls. You can choose limited frames, restricted color palettes, imperfect registration, physical-looking depth, or nonuniform timing even when computing power no longer imposes them.

The next step I’d prioritize is a single interactive prototype that lets us switch between historically faithful reconstruction, modern equivalent, and cross-medium mutation, keeping a record of which transformations actually work.

That’s how Pantheon becomes more than a collection of artistic references. It becomes an instrument for discovering new artistic mechanisms from old ones.

The objective isn’t to recreate the film. It’s to recover the machinery of invention—and invent with it again.